                        Patch Metadata - Gen 40/50 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/10                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ two_stage_dp_corridor                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the continuous gradient-descent optimizer with a two-stage discrete dynamic       │
│                          │ programming solver plus narrow corridor refinement.                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Why this should improve the score:                                                        │
│                          │ 1. The current solution already keeps F1 and F2 fairly good, but F3 is very high, which   │
│                          │ suggests the optimizer is not finding a good global tradeoff with the base-station        │
│                          │ attraction.                                                                               │
│                          │ 2. Dynamic programming is a better fit here because the path is sampled at fixed          │
│                          │ x-locations and the objective can be decomposed into local node/edge costs.               │
│                          │ 3. Stage 1 searches globally over a moderate y-grid using a cost that simultaneously      │
│                          │ balances:                                                                                 │
│                          │    - edge length,                                                                         │
│                          │    - school proximity penalty,                                                            │
│                          │    - base-station signal-drop penalty,                                                    │
│                          │    - curvature/smoothness.                                                                │
│                          │ 4. Stage 2 then performs the recommended local refinement: build a narrow path-centered   │
│                          │ corridor around the first DP result and rerun DP with finer y resolution, reducing        │
│                          │ quantization error much better than post-hoc smoothing.                                   │
│                          │ 5. The path prior is explicitly biased to bend away from the school at (40, 20) and       │
│                          │ toward the base at (70, -30), matching the task guidance while still letting DP optimize  │
│                          │ the final tradeoff.                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This keeps the code deterministic, smooth, and fast enough for evaluation.                │
