│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ analytical_gaussian_spline_solver                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The algorithm uses an **Analytical Gaussian Spline Solver**. Instead of applying          │
│                          │ iterative physical forces or simple heuristics, it constructs the trajectory as a linear  │
│                          │ interpolation baseline plus a linear combination of two "pinned" Gaussian basis           │
│                          │ functions.                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Pinned Gaussian Basis**: It uses Gaussian kernels that are mathematically forced to  │
│                          │ be exactly zero at the start and end points of the flight. This ensures the boundary      │
│                          │ conditions (reaching the destination) are always met with $C^\infty$ smoothness,          │
│                          │ minimizing the flying distance (F1).                                                      │
│                          │ 2. **2x2 Analytical Solution**: It identifies two target displacements: one at the        │
│                          │ school's X-coordinate (to stay 35 units away from the noise source) and one at the base   │
│                          │ station's X-coordinate (to reach target signal strength). It then sets up and solves a    │
│                          │ 2x2 system of linear equations to find the exact amplitudes for the school-repulsion and  │
│                          │ base-attraction kernels.                                                                  │
│                          │ 3. **Optimized Parameters**: It uses relatively broad kernels ($\sigma = 22\%$ of the     │
│                          │ total span) to ensure the path doesn't have sharp turns, which keeps the total distance   │
│                          │ (F1) efficient while satisfying both spatial constraints (F2 and F3).                     │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
